course: dictionary
lesson: 1117
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. Breach can mean a broken rule, a hole in a wall, or a whale leaping out of the water. «The team broke the rules, and the referee called it a breach of the rules.» Here a breach is a broken rule.
explain: «A storm made a breach in the sea wall, and water rushed into the town.» Now breach is a gap or hole. The clues are storm, sea wall and water rushing in. Always ask: what is this sentence about? Rules, walls, computers or whales? Then try that meaning in the sentence and see if it makes sense.
fact: Breach (noun) means breaking a rule, law, promise or agreement, as in «a breach of the rules».
fact: Breach (noun) can also mean a gap or hole in a wall, dam or defence.
fact: To breach (verb) means to break through something, as in «The sea breached the wall».
fact: A data breach is when private information is stolen or leaked, so people may need to change their passwords.
fact: «Breaching» means leaping up out of the water, as whales do. Scientists think whales do it to signal, show off or knock off skin pests.
exercise: «The sea breached the wall during the storm.» What does breached mean? | Fell asleep under | *Broke through | Painted over | Watched carefully
exercise: «Ben made a breach of trust when he shared Sam's secret.» What is a breach of trust? | *Breaking someone's trust | Winning a race | Keeping a promise | Feeding a pet
exercise: «The whale breached right beside the boat.» What is the whale doing? | Singing to the crew | *Leaping up out of the water | Hiding behind a rock | Sleeping at the bottom of the sea
test: «The hackers caused a data breach, and thousands of passwords were stolen.» What is a data breach? | *Private information being stolen or leaked | A new game for computers | Cleaning up a computer | A loud noise in the night
test: «The workers found a breach in the dam, a hole where water was pouring through.» What does breach mean here? | A loud bang | A rule that was broken | A happy party | *A gap or hole in a wall or dam
exam: «Scientists think whales breach to signal, show off or knock off skin pests.» Which meaning of breach fits here? | Breaking through a wall | *Leaping up out of the water | Breaking a rule | A gap in a sea defence
